          ],
          "level": 1,
          "content": "Coin Conjoin"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.223,
            0.849,
            0.296
          ],
          "content": "Arrange 10 coins in a triangle as shown in the figure below on the left. The task is to turn the triangle upside down by moving one coin at a time. How many moves are needed? What is the minimum number of moves?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.297,
            0.848,
            0.336
          ],
          "content": "A triangle of 3 coins can be inverted (turned upside down) with a single move, and a triangle of 6 coins can be inverted by moving 2 coins."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.238,
            0.346,
            0.763,
            0.52
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_22_image_4.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.536,
            0.848,
            0.61
          ],
          "content": "The 10-coin triangle can be flipped with just 3 moves; did you figure out how? Find out the minimum possible moves needed to flip the next bigger triangle having 15 coins. Try the same for bigger triangular numbers."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.609,
            0.848,
            0.648
          ],
          "content": "Is there a simple way to calculate the minimum number of coin moves needed for any such triangular arrangement?"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.036,
            0.666,
            0.906,
            0.965
          ],
